  abstract={This paper outlines the architecture and a real-life implementation of an in-house VCS built out of first principles with the help of Node.js and a plain file storage model. The system provides some of the fundamental features usually provided by more established tools, such as Git, such as staging files, managing commit history, branching, merging, and synchronizing with a remote repository. It is provided with a command-line interface (CLI) to perform local tasks and with a custom remote server to provide the possibility to push and pull. The specific focus is put on merge conflicts management, rebase operation, and file states maintenance when swapping branches. In contrast to traditional VCS systems, this system was designed to be educational and easy to understand, being structured in a modular, educational and easy-to-read way, which makes it an effective platform to learners and researchers studying distributed systems, file tracking systems, and collaborative development systems. To assess the correctness, the execution time and the memory efficiency of the main operations, synthetic and real-life data sets (between 100 and 10,000 files) were used as performance and functional testing. The findings validate the effectiveness and feasibility of the suggested system as the possible alternative of lightweight and accessible systems that can be used in instruction and small-scale development.},
