  year={2024},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1-4},
  abstract={Medical Visual Question Answering is designed for supporting clinical diagnosis by leveraging advanced language and visual comprehension ability. While Large Vision-Language Models offer medical visual question answering powerful generative and interpretative capabilities, they encounter significant computational challenges due to quadratic complexity that scales with sequence length, especially as image embeddings dominate the input sequence. To address this, we propose an Image Rearrangement algorithm, which reduces the number of image embeddings with minimal impact on model performance. The Image Rearrangement algorithm aggregates similar image embeddings based on their relative alignment to text embeddings. We evaluate the Image Rearrangement algorithm method across three distinct datasets using two medical visual question answering models, observing that the Image Rearrangement algorithm achieves substantial computational savings while maintaining diagnostic accuracy.},
